\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Allocation matters, but selection matters more. Median hit-rate improvement of \texttt  {adaptive-e64} over global LRU and remaining Belady-style future-informed headroom across the 14 in-sample workload/split cells for each model and memory budget; whiskers show the full observed range. Layer-aware allocation becomes increasingly valuable under stronger memory pressure, especially for the 30B model. Nevertheless, the remaining future-informed selection headroom is substantially larger in every tested model/budget condition. The Belady-style reference is not an achievable online policy and is used only to quantify residual selection opportunity.}}{10}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:allocation-selection}{{3}{10}{Allocation matters, but selection matters more. Median hit-rate improvement of \texttt {adaptive-e64} over global LRU and remaining Belady-style future-informed headroom across the 14 in-sample workload/split cells for each model and memory budget; whiskers show the full observed range. Layer-aware allocation becomes increasingly valuable under stronger memory pressure, especially for the 30B model. Nevertheless, the remaining future-informed selection headroom is substantially larger in every tested model/budget condition. The Belady-style reference is not an achievable online policy and is used only to quantify residual selection opportunity}{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Workload drift does not imply useful fast adaptation. Panel (a) shows that Jensen--Shannon divergence between routing distributions is substantially larger across workload boundaries than within segments for both evaluated MoEs. Panel (b) compares the gain of a fixed allocation equal to the controller's mean allocation with the gain of the tested controller. The result confirms workload-dependent routing drift but falsifies the temporal mechanism of the current \texttt  {adaptive-e64} controller; it does not establish that dynamic adaptation is ineffective in general.}}{10}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:workload-drift}{{4}{10}{Workload drift does not imply useful fast adaptation. Panel (a) shows that Jensen--Shannon divergence between routing distributions is substantially larger across workload boundaries than within segments for both evaluated MoEs. Panel (b) compares the gain of a fixed allocation equal to the controller's mean allocation with the gain of the tested controller. The result confirms workload-dependent routing drift but falsifies the temporal mechanism of the current \texttt {adaptive-e64} controller; it does not establish that dynamic adaptation is ineffective in general}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Interpretable prediction of token latency. Measured versus leave-one-configuration-out predicted latency for the five preregistered 30B memory configurations. The dashed diagonal denotes perfect prediction. Median absolute percentage error is 2.25\%, below the preregistered 15\% criterion. The result supports the predictive usefulness of the latency decomposition over the tested configurations, without separately identifying every physical latency component.}}{11}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:performance-model}{{5}{11}{Interpretable prediction of token latency. Measured versus leave-one-configuration-out predicted latency for the five preregistered 30B memory configurations. The dashed diagonal denotes perfect prediction. Median absolute percentage error is 2.25\%, below the preregistered 15\% criterion. The result supports the predictive usefulness of the latency decomposition over the tested configurations, without separately identifying every physical latency component}{figure.caption.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Reuse transitions and performance regimes are not universal residency thresholds. Panel (a) shows the 30B extreme-pressure result normalized to the 1.5\,GiB baseline: expert-level reuse collapses substantially faster than generation throughput, and execution continues below the one-token expert footprint. Panel (b) shows the matched-pressure comparison: the preregistered P1$\rightarrow $P2 pressure-response exponents are $\beta _{30}=0.476$ and $\beta _{80}=0.495$ ($\Delta \beta =+0.019$), so the models are not distinguished by the primary explanandum. Open markers show the secondary P3 condition.}}{11}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pressure-regimes}{{6}{11}{Reuse transitions and performance regimes are not universal residency thresholds. Panel (a) shows the 30B extreme-pressure result normalized to the 1.5\,GiB baseline: expert-level reuse collapses substantially faster than generation throughput, and execution continues below the one-token expert footprint. Panel (b) shows the matched-pressure comparison: the preregistered P1$\rightarrow $P2 pressure-response exponents are $\beta _{30}=0.476$ and $\beta _{80}=0.495$ ($\Delta \beta =+0.019$), so the models are not distinguished by the primary explanandum. Open markers show the secondary P3 condition}{figure.caption.16}{}}
